WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.500
- You've said that the
meaning of life is to win.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.050
If you look five years into the future,

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:13.493
what does winning look like?

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:16.350
- So...

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:22.650
There's a lot of,

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:25.210
I can go into like technical depth

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.857
to what I mean by that, to win.

00:00:27.857 --> 00:00:29.310
It may not mean,

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.820
I was criticized for that in the comments,

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.040
like doesn't this guy
want to save the penguins

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:34.606
in Antarctica or like?

00:00:34.606 --> 00:00:37.460
Look man, listen to what I'm saying.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.960
I'm not talking about I
have a yacht or something.

00:00:41.087 --> 00:00:43.100
I am an agent.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:47.230
I am put into this world and

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:49.180
I don't really know what my purpose is.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.860
But if you're an intelligent agent

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:53.990
and you're put into a world,

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:55.750
what is the ideal thing to do?

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:57.390
Well, the ideal thing mathematically,

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.680
you can go back to Schmidt
Hoover theories about this

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.070
is to build a compressive
model of the world,

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:04.440
to build a maximally compressive,

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.530
to explore the world

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:07.610
such that your exploration function

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:11.520
maximizes the derivative
of compression of the past.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.300
Schmidt Hoover has a paper about this.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.630
And like I took that kind of

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:16.403
as like a personal goal function.

00:01:17.525 --> 00:01:18.740
So when I mean to win,

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.290
I mean like,

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.640
maybe this is religious

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.910
but like I think that in the future

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:25.640
I might be given a real purpose

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.160
or I may decide this purpose myself

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:28.730
and then at that point,

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:29.890
now I know what the game is

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:30.810
and I know how to win.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:31.643
I think right now

00:01:31.643 --> 00:01:33.330
I'm still just trying to
figure out what the game is

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.551
but once I know.

00:01:34.551 --> 00:01:39.000
- So you have imperfect information,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.180
you have a lot of uncertainty
about the reward function

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.367
and you're discovering it

00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:43.410
but the purpose is--

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.290
- [George] That's a better way to put it.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.010
- The purpose is to maximize it

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:50.520
while you have a lot of
uncertainty around it.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:51.950
And you're both reducing that certainty

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.341
and maximizing it at the same time.

00:01:54.341 --> 00:01:57.015
So that's at the technical level.

00:01:57.015 --> 00:01:59.990
- If you believe in the universal prior,

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:01.950
what is the universal reward function?

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.860
That's the better way to put it.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.230
- So that win is interesting.

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:09.820
I think I speak for
everyone in saying that

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:14.470
I wonder what that reward
function is for you

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:17.820
and I look forward to seeing that

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.590
in five years, in 10 years.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.230
I think a lot of people, including myself,

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:22.400
are cheering you on man.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.460
So I'm happy you exist

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.163
and I wish you the best of luck.

